---
.title = "Topologies on Free Products",
.date = .date("2026-08-11T20:30:00-04:00"),
.draft = false,
.layout = "post.shtml",
---

In [a previous post]($link.page('blog/topologies-for-groups')),
I introduced a set of axioms for identity neighborhoods
in topological groups.
In general, many of the familiar categorical constructions
for topological groups appear to me to be extremely poorly understood so far.
Some of this appears to me to be intrinsic to the subject,
but plenty of it does not.

For example, in this post I want to describe a collection of group topologies
on the free product [`G * H`]($mathtex) of topological groups
[`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex), and determine which one
makes the free product into the categorical coproduct in the category
of topological groups.

# Free Products, A Refresher

If [`S`]($mathtex) is a set (called an "alphabet"),
the "free monoid" [`S^*`]($mathtex) over [`S`]($mathtex),
also sometimes called the collection of "words" or "strings" in [`S`]($mathtex)
is the collection of finite sequences [`s_1s_2\ldots s_n`]($mathtex),
where each [`s_i \in S`]($mathtex).
Concatenation of strings is an associative, typically noncommutative  binary operation
on [`S^*`]($mathtex) with a two-sided identity, namely the empty string.
(The operation, its associativity and identity are what make [`S^*`]($mathtex)
deserve the appellation "monoid".)

In general, [`S^*`]($mathtex) is not a group,
because there is no inverse to concatenation.
However, when [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex) are groups,
there is an equivalence relation on [`(G \cup H)^*`]($mathtex)
whose quotient is a group.
If [`\omega_1`]($mathtex) and [`\omega_2`]($mathtex) are strings
with equivalence classes [`[\omega_1]`]($mathtex) and [`[\omega_2]`]($mathtex)
respectively, the group operation is
```=mathtex
[\omega_1][\omega_2] = [\omega_1\omega_2].
```
In other words, concatenation of strings descends to become the group operation.

Here is the equivalence relation:
if [`g_1 g_2 = g_3`]($mathtex) in [`G`]($mathtex)
or [`h_1 h_2 = h_3`]($mathtex) in [`H`]($mathtex),
we set
```=mathtex
\omega_1 g_1 g_2 \omega_2 \sim \omega_1 g_3 \omega_2 \quad\text{and}\quad
\omega_1 h_1 h_2 \omega_2 \sim \omega_1 h_3 \omega_2,
```
where [`\omega_1`]($mathtex) and [`\omega_2`]($mathtex) are arbitrary strings.
Also if [`1_G`]($mathtex) and [`1_H`]($mathtex) are the identity elements of
[`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex) respectively, we also set
```=mathtex
\omega_1 1_G \omega_2 \sim \omega_1\omega_2 \quad\text{and}\quad
\omega_1 1_H \omega_2 \sim \omega_1\omega_2.
```
Two strings are equivalent if one can be transformed into the other
by a finite collection of moves.

The quotient of [`(G \cup H)^*`]($mathtex)
by the equivalence relation [`\sim`]($mathtex) generated by these four operations
is a group [`G * H`]($mathtex),
which we call the _free product_ of [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex),
where the inverse of a word [`s_1\ldots s_n`]($mathtex)
is represented by [`s_n^{-1}\ldots s_1^{-1}`]($mathtex),
where the inversion of each letter happens in the group
[`G`]($mathtex) or [`H`]($mathtex) as the case may be.

This equivalence relation is extremely nice
from a theory of computation standpoint, by the way.
Every word can be represented uniquely by a word which cannot be shortened
by any of these four operations,
and such words can be recognized by a finite state automaton
with four states:
**START**, **G**, **H** and **REJECT.**
Only the **REJECT** state is not an accept state,
and the start state is **START.**
From **START,** every nonidentity element of [`G`]($mathtex) takes you to **G**,
and every nonidentity element of [`H`]($mathtex) takes you to **H**.
At **G** nonidentity elements of [`H`]($mathtex) take you to **H**
and conversely at **H**, nonidentity elements of [`G`]($mathtex) take you to **G**.
All other possibilities take you to **REJECT**.

