Provided you know how to compute in [`G`]($mathtex)
and [`H`]($mathtex),
you can adapt the above automaton to help you do computations in [`G * H`]($mathtex).
A "greedy algorithm", for example, which works by seeing where in the word
we move to **REJECT** and performing corresponding one of our four
basic operations which reduces word length and trying again
will run in more or less linear time.

Free products of nontrivial groups [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex)
are always infinite, nonabelian (i.e. the group operation is noncommutative)
and contain canonical copies of both [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex)
as (non-normal) subgroups.

The free product satisfies a "universal property":
If [`K`]($mathtex) is a group and [`G \to K`]($mathtex)
and [`H \to K`]($mathtex) are homomorphisms,
there exists a unique homomorphism
[`G * H \to K`]($mathtex)
such that the restriction of this homomorphism
to the canonical copy of [`G`]($mathtex) or [`H`]($mathtex) respectively
gives back the homomorphisms we started with.

# The Bass-Serre Tree

The abstract group [`G * H`]($mathtex)
acts on a tree called the Bass-Serre tree
with one orbit of edges and two orbits of vertices.
The edges of the tree correspond to elements of the group,
and vertices of the tree correspond to cosets
of the canonical copies of [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex).
The edge [`g`]($mathtex) connects the vertices
[`g G`]($mathtex) and [`g H`]($mathtex).
The action of [`G * H`]($mathtex)
is multiplication in the labels.
The stabilizer of the vertex [`g H`]($mathtex),
for example, is the conjugate [`g H g^{-1}`]($mathtex)
of the subgroup [`H`]($mathtex) in [`G * H`]($mathtex).

It is perhaps not immediate to see that the graph so described is a tree;
this is part of a very beautiful subject called Bass-Serre theory and
named for Jean Pierre Serre, who wrote a book _Trees_ on the subject,
and Hyman Bass who was his grad student at the time,
contributed to the theory from the beginning and continued to develop
the subject for some time after.

At the time of this writing, Serre is 99 years old
and posted a math paper to the arXiv within the past two years.

# Some Topologies

In [the previous post alluded to earlier]($link.page('blog/topologies-for-groups')),
we found a set of axioms characterizing group topologies
in terms of their identity neighborhoods.
In short, identity neighborhoods are closed under finite intersections,
inversion, multiplication, and conjugation.

Supposing [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex) are topological groups,
we are interested in group topologies on [`G * H`]($mathtex)
such that the canonical inclusions of [`G`]($mathtex)
and [`H`]($mathtex) are embeddings.
To this end, we must allow any identity neighborhood
in [`G`]($mathtex) or [`H`]($mathtex) to be at least contained in
an identity neighborhood of [`G * H`]($mathtex).
The challenges, however,
are finite intersections between identity neighborhoods
in [`G`]($mathtex) with those in [`H`]($mathtex),
as well as ensuring that conjugates of identity neighborhoods are identity neighborhoods.

Thinking about it this morning,
the problem feels very similar to the question of topologizing
Cartesian products of spaces.
I have three families of sets,
[`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex), [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex) and [`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex),
each of which satisfies the axioms and induces a different
group topology on [`G * H`]($mathtex).

First, a little notation:
let [`T`]($mathtex) be the Bass-Serre tree from above
and [`v`]($mathtex) a vertex,
and let [`\mathop{\mathrm{Stab}}(v)`]($mathtex) be its stabilizer.
As we saw above, [`v`]($mathtex)
corresponds to a coset of the form [`g G`]($mathtex) or [`g H`]($mathtex),
and we may choose a unique preferred representative of this coset
by requiring that the reduced word representing [`g`]($mathtex)
either ends with an letter in [`H`]($mathtex) in the case of [`gG`]($mathtex)
or with a letter in [`G`]($mathtex) in the case of [`gH`]($mathtex).
Call this element [`g_v`]($mathtex).
In all cases below, let [`U_v`]($mathtex) denote an identity neighborhood
in [`\mathop{\mathrm{Stab}}(v)`]($mathtex).

```=mathtex
\mathcal{P} = \left\{
\bigcup_{v \in T} U_v :
U_v = \mathop{\mathrm{Stab}}(v) \text{ for all but finitely many } v
\right\}
```
I called this [`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex) because
its definition is reminiscent to me of the _product_ topology
on a Cartesian product.
```=mathtex
\mathcal{C} = \left\{
\bigcup_{v \in T} U_v :
U_v = g_v U_G g_v^{-1} \text { or } g_v U_H g_v^{-1}
\text{ for fixed } U_H \subset H \text{ and } U_G \subset G
\right\}
```
The identity neighborhoods feel a bit like "cylinders",
since you fix once and for all [`U_H`]($mathtex)
and [`U_G`]($mathtex) and just conjugate them around,
hence why I chose the name [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex).
```=mathtex
\mathcal{B} = \left\{
\bigcup_{v \in T} U_v 
\right\}
```
Beyond being open, there are no constraints here;
one picks a separate identity neighborhood for each [`v`]($mathtex).
This topology felt most similar to the _box_ topology on a Cartesian product,
hence the name [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex).

A little elementary set theory should convince you
that each element of [`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex)
contains an element of [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex),
and that each element of [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex)
is an element of [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex),
so of the three resulting topologies,
[`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex) is the _finest,_
meaning it has the _most_ open sets,
while [`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex) is the _coarsest,_
having the fewest open sets.

Since the intersection of [`\mathop{\mathrm{Stab}}(v)`]($mathtex)
with [`\mathop{\mathrm{Stab}}(w)`]($mathtex) is trivial (i.e. just the identity)
when [`v \ne w`]($mathtex),
you can work term by term to see that if [`U`]($mathtex)
and [`V`]($mathtex) belong to one of the collections above,
then so do [`U^{-1}`]($mathtex), [`g U g^{-1}`]($mathtex) for any [`g`]($mathtex)
and [`U \cap V`]($mathtex).
Likewise, for each [`U`]($mathtex) there is a [`W`]($mathtex)
such that [`W^2 \subset U`]($mathtex).
Therefore each of the collections
[`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex),
[`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex)
and [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex) produces a group topology on [`G * H`]($mathtex).

For each of these topologies,
the canonical inclusions of [`G`]($mathtex)
and [`H`]($mathtex) are embeddings.
Also for each of these topologies,
one can show that if [`\Phi\colon G \to K`]($mathtex)
and [`\Psi\colon H \to K`]($mathtex)
are continuous homomorphisms of topological groups,
the induced homomorphism [`G * H \to K`]($mathtex)
is continuous.

The idea I have for this is to begin by supposing that
[`g \in G`]($mathtex) and [`h \in H`]($mathtex),
and considering an identity neighborhood [`\Phi(g)\Psi(h)W`]($mathtex)
in [`K`]($mathtex).
By continuity of multiplication in [`K`]($mathtex),
there are identity neighborhoods [`V_g`]($mathtex) and [`V_h`]($mathtex)
in [`K`]($mathtex) such that
[`\Phi(g)V_g \Psi(h)V_h \subset \Phi(g)\Psi(h)W`]($mathtex).
By continuity of [`\Phi`]($mathtex) and [`\Psi`]($mathtex),
there are identity neighborhoods
[`U_g \subset G`]($mathtex) and [`U_h \subset H`]($mathtex)
such that
[`\Phi(g U_g)\Psi(g U_h) \subset \Phi(g) V_g \Psi(h)V_h`]($mathtex).
We can then use [`U_g`]($mathtex)
and [`U_h`]($mathtex)
to define elements of the desired family of identity neighborhoods.
Some careful induction should complete the proof.

# Differentiating the topologies

> **Exercise.**
> The canonical homomorphism [`G * H \to G \times H`]($mathtex),
> where the latter has the product topology,
> is continuous for the [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex)-topology
> (and hence the [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)-topology)
> but in general not for the [`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex)-topology.

The point, you should convince yourself in doing the exercise,
is that the preimage of the basic identity neighborhood
[`U \times V`]($mathtex) contains the element of [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex)
determined by [`U`]($mathtex) and [`V`]($mathtex),
but contains no element of [`\mathcal{P}`]($mathtex).

It's fairly obvious that the [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)-topology
is in general genuinely finer than the [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex)-topology,
but I'm not aware of a universal construction that lets you readily see this.
That is, I don't know of a topological group [`K`]($mathtex)
not isomorphic to [`G * H`]($mathtex)
equipped with homomorphisms from the factors
for which the abstract homomorphism [`G * H \to K`]($mathtex)
is continuous for the [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)-topology
but not for the [`\mathcal{C}`]($mathtex)-topology.
If you can think of one,
[email me!](mailto:robbie.lyman@rutgers.edu), I'd love to hear about it.

> **Proposition.**
> The [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)-topology is the finest group topology
> on [`G * H`]($mathtex) for which the inclusion maps [`G \to G * H`]($mathtex)
> and [`H \to G * H`]($mathtex) are continuous.
> It is therefore the coproduct of [`G`]($mathtex) and [`H`]($mathtex)
> in the category of topological groups.

_Proof._
Suppose that [`\tau`]($mathtex) is a group topology
on [`G * H`]($mathtex) as in the statement.
We show that an arbitrary identity neighborhood in [`\tau`]($mathtex)
contains a [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)-identity neighborhood.
This will show that [`\tau`]($mathtex)-open sets are [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)-open,
completing the proof.

If [`U`]($mathtex) is a [`\tau`]($mathtex)-identity neighborhood,
we know that [`U_G = U \cap G`]($mathtex) and [`U_H = U \cap H`]($mathtex)
are identity neighborhoods, as is [`gUg^{-1}`]($mathtex)
for any [`g`]($mathtex).
More generally, if [`g = g_v`]($mathtex) for [`v \in T`]($mathtex),
notice that [`g_v^{-1} U g_v \cap U \ni 1`]($mathtex) is an identity neighborhood,
so we conclude that [`U \supset g_v U_v g_v^{-1}`]($mathtex)
for some identity neighborhood [`U_v \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Stab}}(v)`]($mathtex).
This constructs an element of [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
contained in [`U`]($mathtex). [`\QED`]($mathtex)
